  "post": {
    "caption": "A jeweler's box hid a new mineral. In 1945, Dublin gemologist Richard Taaffe bought a box of old stones, sorted them by color and checked each under a microscope. One mauve stone looked like spinel, which bends light one way, but this stone bent light two ways. London lab tests confirmed a new mineral, named taaffeite in 1951, and it is the first mineral identified from a cut gem. Its colors range from lilac and violet to pink and red, and it comes mostly from Sri Lanka, with a few finds in Tanzania. Early stones now sit in London's Natural History Museum, and even now it ranks among the rarest gemstones.",
